# GenericSingleton

**File:** `src/yasched/utilizing/GenericSingleton.py`

A mixin base class that enforces a single instance per subclass. Any class that
inherits from `GenericSingleton` becomes a singleton: all calls to the constructor
or `get_instance()` return the same object.

---

## Interface

```
GenericSingleton
  ~ _instance: ClassVar[Self | None]    # per-subclass, not per GenericSingleton
  ~ _lock: ClassVar[threading.Lock]     # per-subclass
  --
  + {static} get_instance() -> Self
  ~ {static} _reset_instance() -> None
```

---

## Behavior

- `get_instance()` creates the instance on the first call and returns the cached
  one on all subsequent calls.
- Thread-safe via double-checked locking: the class-level `_lock` prevents two
  threads from both passing the `_instance is None` check simultaneously.
- `_reset_instance()` clears `_instance` so tests can start with a fresh object.
  Must never be called in production code.

---

## Implementation notes

- Implemented via `__new__` override. Subclasses must not override `__new__`
  unless they explicitly call `super().__new__()`.
- `_instance` and `_lock` are `ClassVar`s so each subclass holds its own
  independent singleton — two different subclasses of `GenericSingleton` each
  have their own instance and do not share state.
